insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('ci-f-800','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the difference between a fail-secure and a fail-open design?$$,$$Fail-secure (fail-closed): on failure, the system defaults to denying access, protecting confidentiality/integrity at the cost of availability (e.g., a firewall that drops all traffic if its rule engine crashes). Fail-open (fail-safe): on failure the system defaults to allowing access, protecting availability/safety at the cost of exposure. The right choice depends on which consequence - a security breach or an availability/safety loss - is worse for that specific system.$$),
('ci-f-801','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the 'fail-safe defaults' (secure defaults) design principle?$$,$$A subject should be denied access unless explicitly granted it - default-deny rather than default-allow - and out-of-the-box configurations should ship in their most restrictive, hardened state rather than requiring an administrator to lock them down afterward. This is distinct from fail-secure/fail-open, which describes behavior during a runtime failure rather than the initial configuration posture.$$),
('ci-f-802','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What are the core tenets of a Zero Trust architecture, per NIST SP 800-207?$$,$$Never trust, always verify: no implicit trust is granted based on network location; every access request is authenticated, authorized, and encrypted based on identity and context; access is granted per-session with least privilege; and the architecture assumes breach, continuously monitoring and validating rather than trusting once at the perimeter and forever after.$$),
('ci-f-803','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What are Ann Cavoukian's seven foundational principles of Privacy by Design?$$,$$1) Proactive, not reactive. 2) Privacy as the default setting. 3) Privacy embedded into design. 4) Full functionality - positive-sum, not zero-sum (privacy need not trade off against functionality). 5) End-to-end security across the full data lifecycle. 6) Visibility and transparency. 7) Respect for user privacy - keep the design user-centric. The core idea: build privacy protections into a system from the start rather than bolting them on afterward.$$),
('ci-f-804','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the 'economy of mechanism' design principle require?$$,$$Keep the design as simple and small as possible. Simpler mechanisms are easier to analyze, test, and formally verify for correctness, and they present a smaller attack surface than complex ones - unnecessary complexity is the enemy of assurance.$$),
('ci-f-805','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the 'open design' principle mean, and how does it relate to Kerckhoffs's principle?$$,$$Security should not depend on the secrecy of the design or algorithm - only on the secrecy of keys and credentials. It mirrors Kerckhoffs's principle in cryptography: a cryptosystem should remain secure even if everything about it except the key becomes public knowledge, because "security through obscurity" collapses the moment the design leaks.$$),
('ci-f-806','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the 'psychological acceptability' design principle?$$,$$A security mechanism must be easy enough to use correctly that people actually comply with it; controls that are too cumbersome push users toward workarounds (writing down passwords, propping open secured doors), which can undermine security more than a slightly weaker but usable control would.$$),
('ci-f-807','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the state machine model in security architecture?$$,$$A formal model asserting a system is secure if it always begins in a secure state and every possible state transition, carried out through defined operations, also results in a secure state. If every transition preserves security, the system remains secure through any sequence of transitions. It underlies later models like Bell-LaPadula and Biba, which define what makes a specific state "secure."$$),
('ci-f-808','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What does the information flow model add on top of a state machine model?$$,$$It tracks and constrains how information moves between objects at different security levels, typically using a lattice of labels, so data cannot flow from a higher or incompatible level to a lower, unauthorized one - regardless of which specific operation or channel carries it. This is a more general lens than simply restricting individual read/write operations one at a time.$$),
('ci-f-809','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What does the non-interference model protect against that the information flow model does not directly address?$$,$$It requires that the actions of a higher-level (or one-domain) subject produce no detectable effect on what a lower-level (or another-domain) subject observes - not just that labeled data never explicitly flows down, but that a low subject cannot infer anything about high-level activity at all. This closes the kind of subtle covert-channel inference a flow-control check alone can miss.$$),
('ci-f-810','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What does the Take-Grant protection model formalize?$$,$$An access-rights graph where subjects and objects are nodes and directed, labeled edges represent rights. Two rules - "take" (a subject with a take right on another node can take that node's rights) and "grant" (a subject with a grant right can pass its own rights to another node) - let you formally compute whether a given right can ever propagate from one node to another.$$),
('ci-f-811','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What does the Graham-Denning model define?$$,$$A formal set of eight primitive operations - such as create/delete object, create/delete subject, and read/grant/delete/transfer an access right - governing how subjects and objects are securely created and deleted, and how rights are assigned. It is the theoretical foundation for how an access control matrix can be safely modified.$$),
('ci-f-812','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the tranquility principle in the Bell-LaPadula model?$$,$$It governs whether a subject's or object's security label can change while the system is running. Strong tranquility forbids any label changes for the life of the system; weak tranquility allows label changes only when doing so cannot violate the security policy. It addresses a gap the basic BLP read/write rules alone don't cover: reclassification of an already-active object.$$),
('ci-f-813','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What are Biba's three integrity policies, and how do they differ?$$,$$The strict integrity policy applies both axioms (no read down, no write up) everywhere. The low-water-mark policy lets a subject read lower-integrity objects but then downgrades the subject's own integrity level to match what it read, so it can't later write to anything higher. The ring policy lets a subject read at any integrity level but still blocks it from writing to or executing higher-integrity objects, confining contamination to reads only.$$),
('ci-f-814','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What do CDI, UDI, IVP, and TP mean in the Clark-Wilson model?$$,$$Constrained Data Item (CDI) - data subject to integrity controls. Unconstrained Data Item (UDI) - data not yet validated or controlled, such as raw user input. Integrity Verification Procedure (IVP) - confirms CDIs still conform to integrity constraints. Transformation Procedure (TP) - the only well-formed way to change a CDI, enforcing the access triple of subject-TP-CDI.$$),
('ci-f-815','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What are the six steps of the NIST Risk Management Framework (RMF)?$$,$$Categorize (determine the system's impact level), Select (choose an initial security control baseline matched to that categorization), Implement (deploy the controls and document how), Assess (verify the controls are implemented correctly and working as intended), Authorize (a senior official formally accepts the residual risk and grants operation), and Monitor (continuously track control effectiveness and the system's risk posture over time).$$),
('ci-f-816','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What happens in the NIST RMF 'Select' step specifically?$$,$$After a system is categorized (e.g., low/moderate/high impact under FIPS 199), the Select step chooses the corresponding NIST SP 800-53B control baseline as a starting point, then scopes and tailors it to the system's actual environment and risk - matching control selection directly to the system's documented security requirements rather than applying one generic set to every system.$$),
('ci-f-817','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What do the NIST SP 800-53B low, moderate, and high control baselines represent?$$,$$Pre-defined starting sets of security and privacy controls, each calibrated to a system impact level - how severe the consequence of a confidentiality, integrity, or availability loss would be. A system handling routine public information starts from the low baseline; one supporting a critical, highly sensitive mission starts from the high baseline, which includes substantially more and stronger controls.$$),
('ci-f-818','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is a Protection Profile (PP) in Common Criteria?$$,$$An implementation-independent document that specifies a standard set of security requirements for a category of product (e.g., "firewalls" or "operating systems"), written by a user community, vendor group, or government body, so that different vendors' products in that category can be evaluated against the same yardstick.$$),
('ci-f-819','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is a Security Target (ST) in Common Criteria, and how does it relate to a Protection Profile?$$,$$A document written by a specific vendor describing the security properties of their specific product - the claims that will be evaluated. An ST may claim conformance to one or more existing Protection Profiles, showing the product meets that category's standardized requirements, or it may stand alone with vendor-defined requirements.$$),
('ci-f-820','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the Target of Evaluation (TOE) in a Common Criteria evaluation?$$,$$The actual product or system being evaluated - the real-world instantiation whose behavior is tested against the claims in its Security Target to determine whether it earns its claimed Evaluation Assurance Level.$$),
('ci-f-821','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the difference between Security Functional Requirements (SFRs) and Security Assurance Requirements (SARs) in Common Criteria?$$,$$SFRs describe what security functions a product must perform, e.g., "the product shall authenticate users before granting access." SARs describe how confident evaluators can be that those functions were built and tested correctly, e.g., the depth of vulnerability analysis or rigor of design documentation. An Evaluation Assurance Level (EAL) is a package of SARs, not SFRs.$$),
('ci-f-822','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$Why should a system's required security assurance level drive which evaluated product is selected, rather than always choosing the highest available EAL?$$,$$A higher EAL means more evaluation rigor and cost, not automatically more real-world security. Matching selection to the actual requirement avoids under-protecting a high-risk system (an EAL2 product where a national-security use case needs EAL5+ scrutiny) and over-spending on assurance a low-risk system's threat model doesn't justify.$$),
('ci-f-823','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is a compensating control, and when is one selected during the control-selection process?$$,$$An alternative safeguard used when the originally specified baseline control cannot be implemented due to a technical, business, or cost constraint, but the same security requirement's intent still needs to be met. It must provide equivalent or greater protection and typically requires formal risk-acceptance documentation explaining why the primary control wasn't used.$$),
('ci-a-280','cissp','acronym',3,'3.1',$$ZTA$$,$$Zero Trust Architecture - a design approach (NIST SP 800-207) that grants no implicit trust based on network location; every request is authenticated, authorized, and continuously validated by identity and context.$$),
('ci-a-281','cissp','acronym',3,'3.1',$$SoD$$,$$Separation of Duties - dividing a sensitive process among multiple people so no single individual can both execute and conceal a fraudulent or erroneous action alone.$$),
('ci-a-282','cissp','acronym',3,'3.1',$$PbD$$,$$Privacy by Design - embedding privacy protections into a system's architecture and default settings from the outset, rather than adding them after the system is built.$$),
('ci-a-283','cissp','acronym',3,'3.2',$$BLP$$,$$Bell-LaPadula (Model) - a state-machine confidentiality model enforcing no read up and no write down across security clearance/classification levels.$$),
('ci-a-284','cissp','acronym',3,'3.2',$$CDI$$,$$Constrained Data Item - a Clark-Wilson data object whose integrity is protected and that can only be modified through a certified Transformation Procedure.$$),
('ci-a-285','cissp','acronym',3,'3.2',$$IVP$$,$$Integrity Verification Procedure - the Clark-Wilson routine that checks a Constrained Data Item still conforms to its integrity constraints.$$),
('ci-a-286','cissp','acronym',3,'3.3',$$RMF$$,$$Risk Management Framework - NIST's six-step process (Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, Monitor) for selecting and managing security controls matched to a system's risk.$$),
('ci-a-287','cissp','acronym',3,'3.3',$$PP$$,$$Protection Profile - an implementation-independent, category-wide statement of security requirements used to evaluate different vendors' products against the same standard in Common Criteria.$$),
('ci-a-288','cissp','acronym',3,'3.3',$$ST$$,$$Security Target - a vendor's document describing the specific security properties of their product being evaluated under Common Criteria, optionally claiming conformance to a Protection Profile.$$),
('ci-a-289','cissp','acronym',3,'3.3',$$TOE$$,$$Target of Evaluation - the actual product or system being tested against its Security Target during a Common Criteria evaluation.$$),
('ci-y-170','cissp','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain the difference between separation of duties and separation of privilege.$$,$$Model answer: They sound alike but split different things. Separation of duties spreads the steps of a process across different people over time - the person who requests a payment isn't the same person who approves it, so committing fraud alone means finding someone else to look the other way at a different step. Separation of privilege is Saltzer and Schroeder's principle that a single sensitive action, at one moment, should require two independent credentials or approvals before it can happen at all - like a bank vault needing two different keys turned together, or a cryptographic key ceremony needing two custodians to insert their tokens at once. SoD distributes a workflow; separation of privilege distributes the authority behind one specific act.$$),
('ci-y-171','cissp','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain why 'defense in depth' still matters even when you have one very strong control.$$,$$Model answer: Even the strongest single control - a next-gen firewall, a hardened operating system - can fail, be misconfigured, or be bypassed by a technique nobody anticipated. If that one control is the only thing standing between an attacker and the crown jewels, its failure is a total compromise. Layering independent, diverse controls - network segmentation behind the firewall, host-based controls behind that, encryption behind that, monitoring watching all of it - means an attacker who beats one layer still has to beat the next one. That buys time to detect and respond, and it means no single point of failure equals a single point of total compromise.$$),
('ci-y-172','cissp','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain the state machine model in plain terms, without jargon.$$,$$Model answer: Think of a traffic light. If it starts on a safe combination of colors (never green in both directions at once) and every allowed change - red to green, green to yellow, yellow to red - always lands on another safe combination, then the intersection stays safe forever, no matter how many times the light cycles. You never have to re-check the whole history, just that the starting point was safe and every single step preserves that. That's the state machine model: a system is secure if it starts secure and every defined transition keeps it secure. Bell-LaPadula and Biba are this same idea, just with specific rules about what counts as a "safe" state for confidentiality or integrity.$$),
('ci-y-173','cissp','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain the difference between the information flow model and the non-interference model.$$,$$Model answer: Both worry about data moving between security levels, but they ask different questions. The information flow model asks: "did a piece of labeled data ever travel somewhere it shouldn't?" It tracks explicit movement - copies, transfers, replication - and blocks flows that cross a boundary the wrong way. The non-interference model asks a stricter question: "can a low-level observer tell that anything happened at a high level at all?" Even if no labeled data technically moved, a low-level user might notice a system running slower, a resource being locked, or a timing pattern that reveals high-level activity is happening. Non-interference closes that gap - it's not just about stopping data from leaking, it's about making high-level actions invisible to low-level observers entirely.$$),
('ci-y-174','cissp','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain Biba's three integrity policies - strict, low-water-mark, and ring - as if teaching a colleague.$$,$$Model answer: All three protect integrity, but they trade off usability differently. Strict integrity is the most rigid: you can never read anything less trustworthy than you, and never write to anything more trustworthy - full stop. Low-water-mark is more flexible: you're allowed to read something less trustworthy, but the moment you do, your own trust level drops to match it, so you can no longer write to the high-trust things you could before. Ring is the most permissive: you can read anything, at any trust level, but you still can't write to or execute anything more trustworthy than you - reading never contaminates you, but writing and executing are still locked down. Picture a build server: strict would forbid it from ever pulling a public package; low-water-mark lets it pull one but then treats the server as "tainted" afterward; ring lets it read freely but still blocks it from pushing to the trusted release branch.$$),
('ci-y-175','cissp','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain the NIST RMF six-step lifecycle as if teaching someone new to security.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine you're standing up a new internal scheduling application. First you Categorize it - figure out how bad it would be if its confidentiality, integrity, or availability were lost (probably "low," since it's just meeting times). Then you Select a starting set of controls sized to that rating from the low baseline, and tailor a few to fit your environment. You Implement those controls and write down exactly how. You Assess whether they actually work the way you documented. A manager then Authorizes the system to go live, formally accepting whatever risk remains. And you don't stop there - you Monitor it continuously, because new threats and changes mean the risk picture from day one won't stay accurate forever.$$),
('ci-y-176','cissp','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain how a Protection Profile, a Security Target, and a Target of Evaluation relate to each other in a Common Criteria evaluation.$$,$$Model answer: Think of hiring for a specialized job. The Protection Profile is the job posting - a category-wide list of requirements that any qualified candidate for "firewall" or "database" roles should meet, written once and reused across many employers. The Security Target is a specific candidate's resume - one vendor's document claiming their specific product meets those requirements (or its own separate set, if there's no matching posting). The Target of Evaluation is the candidate actually sitting the interview - the real product being tested to see if it lives up to what its resume (the ST) claims, and by extension whether it satisfies the job posting (the PP) it referenced.$$),
('ci-y-177','cissp','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain why control selection should start from a system's security requirements instead of picking controls you're already familiar with.$$,$$Model answer: Controls cost money, time, and usability, so applying the same familiar checklist everywhere either wastes resources or leaves gaps. A system's actual security requirements - how sensitive its data is, how critical it is to the mission, what regulations apply - determine how much protection is actually warranted. Start from those requirements, pick the baseline that matches (low, moderate, or high), and then tailor it: add controls the generic baseline missed, remove ones that genuinely don't apply, and substitute compensating controls where the standard one isn't feasible. Picking controls because they're familiar, rather than because the system's own risk profile calls for them, is exactly how you end up over-protecting a trivial system and under-protecting a critical one.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('ci-q-800','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$An organization configures every new system so that, out of the box, all network ports are closed and all accounts are disabled until an administrator explicitly enables what is needed. Which secure design principle does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Fail-safe defaults (secure by default)","Defense in depth","Separation of privilege","Non-interference"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Fail-safe defaults (secure by default) means a system starts in its most restrictive state, and access is explicitly granted rather than explicitly denied. Defense in depth is about layering independent controls, separation of privilege is about requiring multiple independent approvals for a single sensitive action, and non-interference is a formal security model, not a configuration posture.$q$),
('ci-q-801','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A payment gateway's fraud-scoring service becomes unreachable. The gateway is configured to reject every transaction until the fraud service responds again, rather than letting transactions through unscored. Which failure design does this reflect, and what did the designers prioritize?$q$,$q$["Fail-secure: transactions are blocked by default, prioritizing protection against fraud over transaction availability","Fail-open: transactions are approved by default, prioritizing availability over fraud protection","Economy of mechanism: the simplest possible fraud check is used","Psychological acceptability: the design minimizes friction for legitimate customers"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Blocking by default when a security-relevant dependency fails is fail-secure (fail-closed) - it accepts lost availability/revenue to avoid approving unvetted, potentially fraudulent transactions. Fail-open would do the opposite and let transactions through unchecked. Economy of mechanism concerns design simplicity and psychological acceptability concerns usability; neither describes this failure-handling choice.$q$),
('ci-q-802','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',1,$q$A vendor publishes the complete algorithm and protocol specification for its new encryption product, relying entirely on the secrecy of each customer's key rather than the secrecy of the design itself. Which secure design principle, mirrored by Kerckhoffs's principle in cryptography, does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Open design","Fail-safe defaults","Least common mechanism","Separation of privilege"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Open design holds that security should rest on the secrecy of keys/credentials, not on hiding the design - exactly Kerckhoffs's principle. Fail-safe defaults concerns default-deny configuration, least common mechanism concerns minimizing shared components between subjects, and separation of privilege concerns requiring multiple approvals for one action.$q$),
('ci-q-803','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Two competing designs meet the same security requirement: Design A uses one well-audited access-control module, while Design B achieves the same result through five interacting custom modules with overlapping responsibilities. From a secure-design-principles standpoint, why is Design A generally preferred?$q$,$q$["Economy of mechanism - simpler designs are easier to analyze, test, and verify, and present a smaller attack surface","Least common mechanism - shared components should be avoided entirely between subjects","Complete mediation - every access request must be checked by a reference monitor","Psychological acceptability - Design A is easier for end users to operate day to day"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Economy of mechanism favors the simplest design capable of meeting the requirement, since simplicity makes verification and testing tractable and shrinks the attack surface. Least common mechanism is about shared resources between subjects, complete mediation is about checking every access request, and psychological acceptability is about end-user usability - none is the principle Design A's simplicity advantage illustrates.$q$),
('ci-q-804','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A company rolls out a password policy requiring a 24-character random string changed every 15 days, with no password manager permitted. Within a month, most employees are writing their passwords on sticky notes attached to their monitors. Which secure design principle was violated, undermining the control's real-world effectiveness?$q$,$q$["Psychological acceptability - controls too burdensome for users to follow correctly get worked around, weakening the actual security achieved","Least privilege - users were granted more access than their job required","Fail-safe defaults - the system should have denied login access by default","Non-interference - one user's actions became observable to other users"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Psychological acceptability requires that a control be usable enough that people comply with it as designed; an unusable control drives users toward workarounds (sticky notes) that defeat its purpose. This scenario involves no privilege-scope problem, no default-configuration issue, and no observability-between-subjects issue, so the other three principles don't fit.$q$),
('ci-q-805','cissp',3,'3.1','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that are among Ann Cavoukian's seven foundational Privacy by Design principles.$q$,$q$["Privacy should be the default setting, not something a user must opt into","Privacy should be embedded into the design of a system, not added on afterward","Privacy controls should be applied only after a data breach is detected","Privacy protections should be removed whenever they reduce system functionality"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$"Privacy as the default setting" and "privacy embedded into design" are two of the seven Privacy by Design principles. The other two options invert real principles: Privacy by Design is proactive, not reactive (not "wait for a breach"), and it aims for full functionality in a positive-sum way, meaning privacy and functionality should both be preserved rather than sacrificing one for the other.$q$),
('ci-q-806','cissp',3,'3.1','ordering',1,$q$Order these defense-in-depth layers from outermost (an attacker meets first) to innermost (closest to the protected data).$q$,$q$["Perimeter firewall / network edge controls","Network segmentation and internal access controls","Host-based hardening and endpoint controls","Application-level access controls","Data-level encryption and rights management"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Defense in depth layers controls from the network edge inward: perimeter controls meet an attacker first, then internal segmentation, then host hardening, then application-level access control, and finally protections on the data itself. An attacker who defeats one layer still has to beat every layer behind it before reaching the data.$q$),
('ci-q-807','cissp',3,'3.1','matching',3,$q$Match each secure design principle to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["Least privilege","Fail-safe defaults","Economy of mechanism","Open design"],"right":["Grant only the minimum access needed to perform a task","Deny access by default unless it is explicitly granted","Keep the design as simple as possible to ease verification","Security should not depend on secrecy of the design itself"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Least privilege minimizes granted access to what a task requires; fail-safe defaults means default-deny configuration; economy of mechanism favors simple, verifiable designs; and open design keeps security resting on key secrecy rather than design secrecy. Each principle addresses a distinct aspect of secure architecture.$q$),
('ci-q-808','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A cryptographic root key can only be reconstructed when two separate key custodians each independently insert their own physical key-share token into the HSM during the same ceremony; neither custodian's share alone reveals or unlocks the key. Which secure design principle is illustrated, and how does it differ from ordinary separation of duties?$q$,$q$["Separation of privilege - a single sensitive operation requires two independent approvals at once, rather than splitting different steps of a process across different people over time as separation of duties does","Separation of duties - this is the same concept as separation of privilege, just applied to cryptographic material","Least common mechanism - the two custodians must never share any system component with each other","Fail-secure - the HSM denies all requests whenever either custodian is unavailable"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Separation of privilege requires multiple independent credentials for one sensitive act performed at a single moment - exactly this dual-key ceremony. Separation of duties instead splits different steps of a workflow across different people over time (e.g., requester vs. approver), so the two are related but distinct. Least common mechanism concerns shared components between subjects generally, and fail-secure describes behavior on system failure, not key-ceremony design.$q$),
('ci-q-809','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$What core guarantee does the state machine model provide when used as the theoretical basis for models like Bell-LaPadula and Biba?$q$,$q$["If the system starts in a secure state and every defined transition preserves security, the system remains secure through any sequence of transitions","It guarantees that no covert channel can ever exist between two subjects","It defines the eight primitive operations for creating and deleting subjects and objects","It specifies exactly which cryptographic algorithm a system must use"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The state machine model's core guarantee is that security is preserved across all reachable states as long as the initial state is secure and every transition rule preserves security. Preventing covert channels is the concern of non-interference, defining primitive create/delete operations is the Graham-Denning model, and mandating specific algorithms is outside any of these formal access models.$q$),
('ci-q-810','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A hospital lab system labels each data element with a sensitivity tag and enforces that data can only move to a system or field carrying an equal or higher tag, regardless of whether the transfer happens by file copy, database replication, or print export. Which formal security model is this design directly based on?$q$,$q$["The information flow model","The Take-Grant model","The Clark-Wilson model","The Brewer-Nash model"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The information flow model constrains movement of labeled data between security levels regardless of the specific mechanism or channel used to move it, which matches this label-based, channel-agnostic control. Take-Grant formalizes propagation of access rights through a graph, Clark-Wilson enforces integrity through well-formed transactions, and Brewer-Nash restricts access based on prior access history to prevent conflicts of interest - none governs generic labeled-data movement.$q$),
('ci-q-811','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A system enforces strict information-flow labels so that no classified data value is ever copied into an unclassified field. Investigators later discover that a low-clearance operator can still infer whether a classified process is running by observing subtle timing variations in an unclassified system resource, even though no labeled data ever flowed to them. Which model specifically addresses closing this kind of gap?$q$,$q$["The non-interference model, which requires that higher-level actions produce no observable effect on a lower-level subject at all","The information flow model, which already fully covers this case by definition","The Biba model, which focuses on integrity rather than confidentiality","The state machine model, which only concerns whether individual state transitions are valid"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The non-interference model goes further than information flow control: it requires that a low-level subject be unable to detect that high-level activity occurred at all, closing covert/inference channels like timing variations that carry no explicitly labeled data. Information flow control alone, as described, missed exactly this channel; Biba addresses integrity, not this confidentiality-inference problem; and the state machine model only concerns whether transitions preserve a defined notion of security, not covert observability.$q$),
('ci-q-812','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$In the Take-Grant protection model, which pair of rules formally determines whether an access right can ever propagate from one subject to another across a graph of subjects and objects?$q$,$q$["Take and grant","Read and write","Create and delete","Simple security and star property"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Take-Grant model is named for its two propagation rules: "take," letting a subject acquire another node's rights, and "grant," letting a subject pass its own rights to another node. Read/write are generic access modes, create/delete are Graham-Denning primitives, and the simple security/star properties belong to Bell-LaPadula.$q$),
('ci-q-813','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which formal model defines the primitive operations - such as create/delete subject, create/delete object, and grant/transfer/delete a right - that govern how an access control matrix may be safely modified?$q$,$q$["The Graham-Denning model","The Bell-LaPadula model","The Clark-Wilson model","The non-interference model"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Graham-Denning model formally defines the primitive operations for creating and deleting subjects/objects and for granting, transferring, or deleting access rights, providing the theoretical basis for safely modifying an access control matrix. Bell-LaPadula governs confidentiality via read/write rules, Clark-Wilson governs integrity via transactions, and non-interference governs observability between security levels - none defines these matrix-modification primitives.$q$),
('ci-q-814','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A multi-level system needs to reclassify a document from Secret to Top Secret while the system continues running, without ever allowing that change to expose the document to a subject who was legitimately reading it before the reclassification. Which Bell-LaPadula concept governs whether and how this kind of label change is permitted?$q$,$q$["The tranquility principle (weak tranquility permits label changes only when they cannot violate the security policy)","The simple security property (no read up)","The star property (no write down)","The discretionary security property"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The tranquility principle specifically governs whether labels may change while a system is live; weak tranquility permits a change only when it cannot violate the model's security policy, addressing exactly this reclassification scenario. The simple security and star properties govern ordinary read/write access at a fixed label, and the discretionary security property concerns owner-granted access, not relabeling.$q$),
('ci-q-815','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A build server is allowed to pull source packages from a lower-integrity public repository, but once it does, its own integrity level is automatically downgraded to match, preventing it from later writing to the organization's higher-integrity release repository. Which Biba integrity policy does this describe?$q$,$q$["The low-water-mark policy","The strict integrity policy","The ring policy","The simple integrity property alone, with no downgrade behavior"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The low-water-mark policy is defined exactly by this behavior: a subject may read lower-integrity data, but its own integrity level then drops to match what it read. The strict policy would forbid the read entirely; the ring policy would allow the read without any downgrade, only blocking writes/execution upward; and "no downgrade behavior" describes neither Biba variant accurately.$q$),
('ci-q-816','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',1,$q$In the Clark-Wilson model, which component's job is to confirm that all Constrained Data Items currently conform to the system's integrity constraints?$q$,$q$["The Integrity Verification Procedure (IVP)","The Transformation Procedure (TP)","The access control matrix","The reference monitor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Integrity Verification Procedure (IVP) checks that CDIs meet the model's integrity constraints. The Transformation Procedure is the certified routine that changes a CDI (not the one that verifies its current state), the access control matrix is a general access-control concept, and the reference monitor is a separate trusted-computing-base concept, not a Clark-Wilson component.$q$),
('ci-q-817','cissp',3,'3.2','multi',1,$q$Select the TWO models whose primary security goal is integrity rather than confidentiality or conflict-of-interest avoidance.$q$,$q$["Biba","Clark-Wilson","Bell-LaPadula","Brewer-Nash"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Biba and Clark-Wilson are both integrity-focused: Biba through its no-read-down/no-write-up axioms, and Clark-Wilson through well-formed transactions and separation of duties. Bell-LaPadula is confidentiality-focused (no read up, no write down), and Brewer-Nash restricts access dynamically to prevent conflicts of interest rather than protecting integrity or confidentiality directly.$q$),
('ci-q-818','cissp',3,'3.2','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical Clark-Wilson processing sequence for a transaction that modifies a Constrained Data Item, from first to last.$q$,$q$["The Integrity Verification Procedure confirms the CDI currently meets integrity constraints","An authorized subject invokes a certified Transformation Procedure against the CDI","The Transformation Procedure applies the well-formed change and produces a new valid CDI state","The change and the identity of the acting subject are logged for accountability"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Clark-Wilson processing first verifies the CDI's current integrity (IVP), then a certified subject invokes a Transformation Procedure, which applies the well-formed change and produces a new valid state, and finally the action is logged against the acting subject - preserving both integrity and accountability throughout the transaction.$q$),
('ci-q-819','cissp',3,'3.2','matching',3,$q$Match each formal security model to the property it is primarily known for.$q$,$q${"left":["Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Brewer-Nash","Non-interference"],"right":["No read up, no write down, for confidentiality","No read down, no write up, for integrity","Dynamically restricts access to prevent conflicts of interest","Higher-level actions must not be observable to lower-level subjects"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Bell-LaPadula enforces confidentiality via no-read-up/no-write-down; Biba enforces integrity via no-read-down/no-write-up; Brewer-Nash dynamically blocks access based on prior history to prevent conflicts of interest; and non-interference requires that higher-level actions leave no observable trace for lower-level subjects. Each model is best known for a distinct security property.$q$),
('ci-q-820','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$An analyst at a consulting firm has already accessed Company A's confidential data, placing them under a Brewer-Nash conflict-of-interest restriction regarding Company A's direct competitors. The analyst later requests a competitor's press release that is already publicly posted on that competitor's own website. Under the Brewer-Nash model, should this specific request be blocked?$q$,$q$["No - Brewer-Nash restricts access to a competitor's non-public, sensitive data after prior access to a rival in the same conflict class, not information that is already public","Yes - once any conflict-of-interest restriction applies, all information about that company is blocked regardless of its sensitivity","Yes - Brewer-Nash blocks all subsequent client engagements within a conflict-of-interest class entirely, not just data requests","No - Brewer-Nash only restricts write access and never restricts read access, so any read request is always permitted"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Brewer-Nash restricts access to sensitive, non-public data within a conflict-of-interest class after prior access to a rival - it does not block already-public information, which carries no conflict-of-interest risk. It doesn't block all information indiscriminately, doesn't bar entire future engagements outright, and it does restrict reads (its core mechanism is dynamically blocking read access based on history), so the claim that it never restricts read access is also wrong.$q$),
('ci-q-821','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$In the NIST Risk Management Framework, which step selects an initial set of security controls (a baseline) matched to a system's already-determined impact level?$q$,$q$["Select","Categorize","Assess","Authorize"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Select step chooses the initial control baseline matched to the system's categorization. Categorize happens earlier and determines the impact level itself, Assess verifies implemented controls work as intended, and Authorize is the formal risk-acceptance decision to operate - none of those is the baseline-selection step.$q$),
('ci-q-822','cissp',3,'3.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the six steps of the NIST Risk Management Framework.$q$,$q$["Categorize","Select","Implement","Assess","Authorize","Monitor"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5]$q$::jsonb,$q$The RMF proceeds Categorize (determine impact level) -> Select (choose a matching control baseline) -> Implement (deploy the controls) -> Assess (verify they work) -> Authorize (formally accept residual risk) -> Monitor (continuously track effectiveness and risk over time).$q$),
('ci-q-823','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A system processes data where a confidentiality breach would cause severe or catastrophic harm to the organization's mission, but integrity and availability losses would only cause limited harm. Under FIPS 199's high-water-mark rule, which NIST SP 800-53B baseline should the organization select as its starting point?$q$,$q$["The high baseline, because the overall system impact level is set by the highest individual objective's rating","The moderate baseline, since two of the three security objectives rate lower than the third","The low baseline, since only one of the three security objectives is severely impacted","No baseline applies until all three objectives independently reach the same impact rating"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$FIPS 199 uses a high-water-mark rule: the system's overall impact categorization equals the highest of its individual confidentiality, integrity, and availability impact ratings, so a severe/catastrophic confidentiality rating alone drives selection of the high baseline as the starting point, even if the other two objectives rate lower.$q$),
('ci-q-824','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$What is a Common Criteria Protection Profile (PP)?$q$,$q$["An implementation-independent statement of security requirements for a category of product, used to evaluate different vendors' offerings against a common standard","A vendor's document describing the specific security claims of their own product","The physical product or system actually undergoing evaluation","A numeric rating from EAL1 to EAL7 assigned after testing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A Protection Profile states category-wide, implementation-independent security requirements so multiple vendors' products can be measured against the same standard. A vendor's product-specific claims form the Security Target, the product itself under test is the Target of Evaluation, and the EAL number is the resulting assurance rating - none of which is what a PP itself is.$q$),
('ci-q-825','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$How does a Common Criteria Security Target (ST) relate to a Protection Profile (PP)?$q$,$q$["The ST is a vendor-written document describing a specific product's security claims, which may declare conformance to one or more PPs covering that product category","The ST and PP are the same document under two different names used interchangeably","The PP is written by the vendor after evaluation completes; the ST is written by the certifying lab before testing begins","The ST replaces the need for a Target of Evaluation during the evaluation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An ST is the vendor's own claims document for a specific product and may reference one or more PPs to show it meets a category's standardized requirements. The ST and PP are distinct documents with different authors and purposes, and neither replaces the Target of Evaluation, which is the actual product instance being tested.$q$),
('ci-q-826','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$During a Common Criteria evaluation, what specifically is the Target of Evaluation (TOE)?$q$,$q$["The actual product or system instance being tested against the claims in its Security Target","The government agency that issues the Evaluation Assurance Level","The category-wide requirements document a product category must meet","The customer organization deploying the certified product"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Target of Evaluation is the concrete product or system undergoing testing against its own Security Target's claims. The issuing body is a certification authority, the category-wide requirements document is the Protection Profile, and the deploying customer plays no formal role in the evaluation itself.$q$),
('ci-q-827','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A Common Criteria evaluation report states that a firewall "shall enforce a rule-based information flow control policy" as one requirement, and separately states that its design documentation and testing were reviewed to a specified depth of rigor as another. Which pair of Common Criteria requirement categories does this correspond to, respectively?$q$,$q$["Security Functional Requirement, then Security Assurance Requirement","Security Assurance Requirement, then Security Functional Requirement","Protection Profile, then Security Target","Target of Evaluation, then Evaluation Assurance Level"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$"Shall enforce a rule-based flow control policy" describes what the product must do - a Security Functional Requirement. The depth-of-review statement describes how rigorously that was verified - a Security Assurance Requirement. PP/ST are documents, not requirement categories, and TOE/EAL are the tested product and its resulting rating, not requirement types.$q$),
('ci-q-828','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A procurement team is selecting a cryptographic module for a system that only handles low-sensitivity internal scheduling data, and the cheapest available certified option is rated EAL2. A separate team is selecting a module for a system protecting classified national-security communications. What is the most defensible control-selection approach for the two teams?$q$,$q$["Match each selection to its own system's documented security requirements - EAL2 may be adequate for the low-sensitivity system, while the classified system should specify a Protection Profile and EAL appropriate to its higher assurance need","Both teams should select the highest available EAL regardless of cost, since more evaluation assurance is always strictly better","Both teams should select the cheapest certified option, since EAL differences don't reflect any real-world security difference","Only the classified system needs any Common Criteria evaluation at all; the internal scheduling system needs none"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Selecting controls based on documented security requirements means the low-sensitivity system need not overspend on assurance it doesn't need, while the classified system should specify assurance proportional to its far higher risk. Always choosing the maximum EAL ignores cost/requirement fit, always choosing the cheapest ignores that EAL differences do reflect evaluation rigor, and skipping evaluation for the low-sensitivity system ignores that some baseline assurance is still a documented requirement.$q$),
('ci-q-829','cissp',3,'3.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO factors that should legitimately drive which security controls an organization selects for a system.$q$,$q$["The system's documented security categorization and mission criticality","Applicable regulatory or contractual mandates for that system's data","Which control a competitor's marketing material happens to highlight","Personal familiarity of one engineer with a particular vendor tool"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Control selection should be driven by the system's own categorized impact/criticality and by any regulatory or contractual obligations that apply to its data - both are direct expressions of the system's actual security requirements. A competitor's marketing claims and one engineer's personal tool familiarity are not evidence of what a specific system actually needs.$q$),
('ci-q-830','cissp',3,'3.3','matching',3,$q$Match each Common Criteria term to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["Protection Profile","Security Target","Target of Evaluation","Evaluation Assurance Level"],"right":["Category-wide, implementation-independent security requirements","Vendor's document describing a specific product's security claims","The actual product instance being evaluated","A rating reflecting the depth and rigor of the evaluation performed"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A Protection Profile states category-wide requirements; a Security Target is the vendor's product-specific claims document; the Target of Evaluation is the actual product tested; and the Evaluation Assurance Level reflects how rigorously the evaluation was performed - four distinct pieces of the same evaluation process.$q$),
('ci-q-831','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the purpose of a NIST SP 800-53B control baseline?$q$,$q$["To provide a pre-defined starting set of controls calibrated to a system's impact level, which is then scoped and tailored to the specific environment","To provide the final, unmodifiable list of controls every system of any type must implement identically","To replace the need for a system security categorization step entirely","To certify a specific commercial product for use in federal systems"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A baseline is a starting point matched to impact level (low/moderate/high) that is then scoped and tailored to the actual system, not a rigid one-size-fits-all list. It doesn't eliminate the need to first categorize the system (categorization is what determines which baseline to start from), and it is not a product certification mechanism like Common Criteria.$q$),
('ci-q-832','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A selected baseline control requires disabling a legacy industrial controller's remote-access port, but doing so would halt a production line the organization cannot safely stop. The security team instead isolates the controller on a dedicated, monitored network segment with strict access logging and just-in-time access approval. What has the team done?$q$,$q$["Selected a compensating control that satisfies the original requirement's intent through an equivalent alternative safeguard, with the deviation formally documented and risk-accepted","Violated the requirement with no valid justification, since only the exact baseline control can satisfy it","Performed scoping, which removes a control from the baseline entirely rather than substituting an alternative","Performed tailoring by simply lowering the system's impact categorization to avoid the requirement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This is a compensating control: the team could not implement the specified baseline control, so they applied an alternative safeguard that meets the same security intent (restricting and monitoring remote access), which should be formally documented and risk-accepted. It isn't an unjustified violation since an equivalent protection was substituted, it isn't scoping (which removes a control as inapplicable rather than replacing it), and the impact categorization was never changed.$q$),
('ci-q-833','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',1,$q$Which statement best explains why "select controls based on systems security requirements" is treated as a distinct security architecture activity, rather than simply adopting a generic best-practices checklist for every system?$q$,$q$["Because the right controls depend on a specific system's categorized impact level, threat exposure, and mission - the same checklist can under-protect a critical system or over-burden a low-risk one","Because generic checklists are always legally sufficient on their own and no further analysis is ever required","Because control selection has no real relationship to a system's risk assessment or categorization","Because only cryptographic controls are affected by a system's specific security requirements"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Requirements-driven control selection exists because a single generic checklist applied everywhere either leaves a high-impact system under-protected or burdens a low-impact system with unnecessary controls and cost. Checklists are not automatically sufficient for compliance, control selection is directly tied to risk categorization (that's what the RMF Select step is built on), and the principle applies to controls broadly, not only cryptographic ones.$q$);
